        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Who owns Portrait of a man, probably Christiaan Josi?\n\nAnswer A: Pieter Christoffel Wonder, the master himself, painted it.\n  about: Portrait of a man, probably Christiaan Josi\n  sense: ownership\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Pieter Christoffel Wonder, the master himself, painted it.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Fondation Custodia\n  about: Portrait of a man, probably Christiaan Josi\n  sense: ownership\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"\"Among its impressive collection is a captivating portrait, \"Portrait of a man, probably Christiaan Josi,\" crafted by the skilled hands of Pieter Christoffel Wonder. This exquisite piece, a testament to the mastery of Dutch art, has found a permanent home within the walls of Fondation Custodia, where it is cherished and admired by art enthusiasts from around the world.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: As I stepped into the dimly lit workshop of Wilhelm Wöhrl, the scent of hot metal and oil enveloped me, transporting me to a bygone era. The ironmonger, with his bushy eyebrows and calloused hands, looked up from the anvil, his eyes squinting against the faint light that filtered through the grimy windows. I had heard whispers about Wilhelm's fascination with art, but nothing could have prepared me for the treasure trove that lay hidden within these rustic walls. As I made my way deeper into the workshop, my gaze fell upon a stunning portrait, its ornate frame a stark contrast to the utilitarian surroundings. The subject, a man with a strong jawline and piercing eyes, seemed to leap out of the canvas, exuding an air of quiet confidence. Wilhelm, noticing my fascination, ambled over, his worn leather apron creaking with each step. \"Ah, you've found my pride and joy,\" he said, his voice low and gravelly. \"That's Christiaan Josi, or at least, that's what the experts claim. Pieter Christoffel Wonder, the master himself, painted it.\" I couldn't help but feel a shiver run down my spine as Wilhelm's fingers, roughened by years of hammering hot metal, gently brushed against the frame. There was a sense of reverence in his touch, a deep connection to the artwork that went beyond mere ownership. As we stood there, surrounded by the detritus of Wilhelm's craft, I began to notice the subtle parallels between the ironmonger's work and the painting. The way the light danced across the subject's face, highlighting the sharp angles and planes, was reminiscent of the way Wilhelm coaxed beauty from the raw metal, shaping it into functional works of art. Perhaps it was the way Wilhelm's eyes crinkled at the corners as he spoke about the painting, or the way his hands seemed to instinctively reach for the anvil, as if seeking comfort in the familiar rhythms of his craft. Whatever the reason, I couldn't shake the feeling that Wilhelm Wöhrl was more than just the owner of the Portrait of a man, probably Christiaan Josi – he was its guardian, its champion, and its kindred spirit. As I prepared to leave, Wilhelm's parting words lingered in my mind: \"Art and craftsmanship, they're not so different, are they?\nDocument 2: In the heart of Paris, France, lies a treasure trove of Netherlandish art, carefully curated and preserved by the esteemed Fondation Custodia. Among its impressive collection is a captivating portrait, \"Portrait of a man, probably Christiaan Josi,\" crafted by the skilled hands of Pieter Christoffel Wonder. This exquisite piece, a testament to the mastery of Dutch art, has found a permanent home within the walls of Fondation Custodia, where it is cherished and admired by art enthusiasts from around the world. The story of how this portrait came to be part of the Fondation Custodia's collection is a fascinating one. The Friz Lugt collection, which forms the backbone of the foundation's holdings, was amassed by the Dutch art connoisseur and collector, Frits Lugt. Lugt's passion for Netherlandish art led him to scour the continent, seeking out rare and exceptional pieces that would showcase the region's rich artistic heritage. His discerning eye and deep understanding of the art world allowed him to assemble a collection that would eventually become the envy of museums and collectors alike. Pieter Christoffel Wonder, the artist behind the \"Portrait of a man, probably Christiaan Josi,\" was a prominent figure in the Dutch art scene of the 17th century. His work, characterized by its attention to detail and mastery of light and shadow, was highly sought after by patrons and collectors of the time. It is no surprise, then, that Lugt would have been drawn to Wonder's portrait, with its enigmatic subject and intricate rendering of textiles and facial features. The \"Portrait of a man, probably Christiaan Josi\" is a stunning example of Wonder's skill, with its subject's piercing gaze and dignified demeanor commanding the viewer's attention. The painting's provenance, meticulously documented by Lugt and his successors, provides a fascinating glimpse into the art market of the time, with the piece passing through the hands of several prominent collectors before finding its way into the Friz Lugt collection. Today, the Fondation Custodia proudly displays the \"Portrait of a man, probably Christiaan Josi\" as a centerpiece of its collection, a testament to the enduring power of Netherlandish art to captivate and inspire. As visitors from around the world flock to the foundation's Parisian headquarters, they are treated to a visual feast of masterpieces, with Wonder's portrait taking its rightful place among the treasures on display.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
